## 2. VECTOR I: POLYGLOT ISA & KERNEL DIVERSITY IN BFT QUORUMS ($\text{HHI} \le 0.15$)

### 2.1 The Mechanistic Mismatch: Enterprise Endpoint Monoculture
Corporate IT departments enforce strict standardization—deploying a single operating system kernel, a single cloud hypervisor, and a single privileged EDR kernel driver across $100\%$ of endpoints. The Herfindahl-Hirschman Index ($\text{HHI}$) of such a fleet approaches unity:
$$\text{HHI} = \sum_{i=1}^M s_i^2 \to 1.0$$
In a monoculture ($\text{HHI} = 1.0$), a single kernel bug or zero-day exploit achieves instant, 100% fleet-wide blast radius.

### 2.2 The Defensive Isomorphism: BFT Quorum Heterogeneity
Applying **Invariant 1 (Ashby's Law of Requisite Variety)** to high-assurance validator, consensus, and key-custody quorums:
1. **Genotype Diversity Bound:** Across a federated BFT quorum of $N$ nodes ($N \ge 3f + 1$, `Invariant 17`), instruction set architectures (ARM64, RISC-V, x86_64) and OS kernels (hardened Linux, OpenBSD, seL4 microkernels) are partitioned such that the maximum share $s_{\max} = \max_i(s_i)$ of any single kernel binary ABI or configuration satisfies $s_{\max} \le 0.15$.
2. **Mathematical HHI Relationship:** Because $\text{HHI} = \sum_{i=1}^M s_i^2 \le s_{\max} \sum_{i=1}^M s_i = s_{\max}$, bounding the maximum single-genotype share to $s_{\max} \le 0.15$ deterministically guarantees that $\text{HHI} \le 0.15$:
   $$s_{\max} \le 0.15 \implies \text{HHI}_{\text{Compute}} = \sum_{i=1}^M s_i^2 \le 0.15 \implies \text{MaxBlastRadius}(\text{CVE}_i) \le 15\%$$
3. **Exploit Containment:** Any zero-day exploit targeting Genotype $i$ compromises at most $15\%$ of the quorum. The unaffected $85\%+$ Byzantine majority isolates and re-flashes the compromised slice automatically without loss of consensus.

