## 6. VECTOR V: EDGE AST GATING VS. M/M/1 SIEM ALERT FATIGUE QUEUEING COLLAPSE (`INVARIANTS 23 & 28`)

### 6.1 Defeating North-South Perimeter Fixation
* **The Flaw:** Traditional security architectures filter inbound North-South traffic while trusting internal East-West LAN traffic and outbound egress—allowing lateral movement once the perimeter is breached.
* **The Fix (`Invariant 7`):** Strict cryptographic capability verification is enforced on all state commits regardless of whether traffic originates externally, on loopback (`127.0.0.1`), or across East-West LAN peers.

### 6.2 Blocking Supply-Chain & Third-Party Proxy SDK Hitchhiking
* **The Flaw:** Attackers bypass firewalls via compromised upstream dependencies, signed driver updates (`SPEC-2026-UNDERWRITER-LIABILITY-RESILIENCE-FALLACY-v6.0`), or third-party consumer proxy SDKs embedded in software that turn local endpoints into exit nodes (**Collateral-Blocking ASN Lock-In**).
* **The Fix (`Invariant 7 & 28`):**
  1. *SHA-256 Dependency Provenance:* All data payloads and code modules lacking cryptographic chain-of-custody attestations are rejected at ingestion.
  2. *Prohibition of Third-Party Exit SDKs:* Sovereign nodes strictly prohibit third-party bandwidth-monetization or exit-proxy SDKs (`Egress_Relay_ThirdParty = 0`).

### 6.3 Bounding Thermodynamic & Resource Exhaustion
* **The Flaw:** Attackers trigger resource-intensive application paths, auto-scaling loops ("Denial of Wallet"), or LLM context saturation.
* **The Fix (`Invariant 14 & 29`):** Hard local energy and context bounds (`Tokens_MaxContext`) enforce pre-committed compute pricing floors before allocating CPU cycles.

### 6.4 Mathematical Elimination of SIEM Alert Fatigue Queue Blowup
* **The Flaw (M/M/1 Queue Blowup):** Enterprise SOCs route raw logs from thousands of endpoints into a centralized queue inspected by human analysts ($\mu_{\text{SOC}}$ reviews/sec). Under alert burst rate $\Lambda = \sum \lambda_k \to \mu_{\text{SOC}}$, the M/M/1 mean analyst wait time $W_{\text{SOC}}$ diverges:
  $$W_{\text{SOC}} = \frac{1}{\mu_{\text{SOC}} - \Lambda} \to \infty$$
  By Little's Law ($L_{\text{SOC}} = \Lambda W_{\text{SOC}}$), the unreviewed SIEM log backlog $L_{\text{SOC}}$ explodes to infinity:
  $$\lim_{\Lambda \to \mu_{\text{SOC}}} L_{\text{SOC}} = \lim_{\Lambda \to \mu_{\text{SOC}}} \frac{\Lambda}{\mu_{\text{SOC}} - \Lambda} = \infty$$
* **The Sovereign Fix ($\mathcal{O}(1)$ Edge Drop Gate, `Invariant 21 & 23`):** Every edge node evaluates incoming state transitions against a compiled AST task graph locally in $\mathcal{O}(1)$ time. Non-compliant tasks are dropped at the edge in $<1\text{ ms}$, ensuring zero central queueing overhead ($\Lambda_{\text{central}} = 0 \implies L_{\text{SOC}} = 0$).

